      "writtenOn": "2018-11-14T21:19:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027ve described the scenarios I\u0027ve been able to think of below for this code, is there a case missing?\n\n- Multiple invocations of virt_wifi_scan_result: impossible. virt_wifi_scan is guarded by the wireless_dev mutex, so there is only ever one caller inside that method. That method is the only place that can schedule virt_wifi_scan_result calls, and it guards itself against doing that by checking if priv-\u003escan_request is null. This requires setting priv-\u003escan_request to NULL at the last possible moment to avoid overlapping runs.\n- wiphy / net_device is taken down: state is protected by virt_wifi_cancel_scan, which is invoked by the network going down callback. It uses cancel_delayed_work_sync which either waits for the scheduled callback to finish if it is already executing, or unschedules the callback if it has not started executing. That leaves it in a definite not-executing state when virt_wifi_cancel_scan interacts with priv-\u003escan_request.\n- Deletion of the net_device / wiphy: The kernel sends devices to the \"down\" state before deleting them. The down transition callback calls virt_wifi_cancel_scan, which guarantees putting the device into a not-scanning state as described above.\n\ncfg80211_scan_done also synchronously deletes the scan request passed in as a parameter, so references to that memory should be cleared afterwards.\nhttps://elixir.bootlin.com/linux/latest/source/include/net/cfg80211.h#L2893\n\"... the scan request is given the driver, and will be valid until passed to cfg80211_scan_done().\"",
      "parentUuid": "4a1d387a_6b994122",
      "revId": "9222f683679b26ff23788e888706451c3b2caafa",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
